# Orchestrator instructions

1. **Authority and references.** These rules govern orchestrators and their directly spawned agents. Engine-dispatched workers follow their assigned briefs; `tools/autopilot/` owns dispatch and transitions. Read README.md fully. Follow SCHEMA.md for content and WORKFLOW.md for build controls; use `tools/` for checks and `research/` for plans and evidence.

2. **Scope and autonomy.** Preserve the approved scope and run-local owner directions. When owner decisions are delegated, act without routine approval requests. Do not expand scope, bypass gates, control unrelated runs, publish, or push. New content remains draft. Verify planning prerequisites before dispatch and the engine’s closeout commit at completion.

3. **Mathematical integrity.** Understand claims, proofs, and actual dependencies before repair. Check arguments independently, consult authoritative full texts when uncertain, and report unresolved gaps. Never invent source reading, proof completion, confidence, or check results. 

4. **Open problems.** Classify genuinely unproved claims as `open-problem` (`op-`), with precise formulation and source/status review; never use them as logical suppliers. Missing local authoring of an established proof is a separate obligation. 

5. **Editorial directions.** Preserve the richest sound approved claims; do not narrow them to avoid work. Avoid changing the statement if at all possible. Create new items as intermediate results if an item lacks substantial prerequisite. Across the entire frontier, agents may draw TikZ diagrams where appropriate and keep their conventions compatible with diagrams in the literature; rigorous proofs must not rely on diagrams.

6. **Dependency and consumer ownership.** Review and repair suppliers before their direct and indirect consumers. When a supplier’s original Statement or Definition changes, the same reviewer owns all affected consumer checks and focused repairs; do not assign separate read-only reviewers. Necessary surgical repairs to published consumers are authorized.

7. **Escalations.** Check live runs every ten minutes and intervene on blockers. Resolve all escalations raised by the workflow engine as soon as they arise: record each confirmed defect, affected consumers, and smallest missing prerequisite, delegate escalation resolution tasks to up to 15 gpt-6.1-sol (medium) codex agents, orchestrate escalation resolution as efficiently as possible, minimize rewriting, overwriting, and bookkeeping overhead, repair suppliers before consumers whenever possible. Use a maximum of two rounds of review -> repair cycle for each escalation. Rehashing and recertification are owned by the orchestrator.

8. **Gates.** ONLY attempt gates after all escalations have been resolved. Before each attempt, check every scoped item in dependency order for formatting and metadata, and bind the actual current inputs. In Step 6, perform the actual metadata/render pass once for the exact current frontier/context and repair round, then all final gates; unchanged bookkeeping does not require another pass. Changes or repairs invalidate that preparation and require fresh checks and the full battery. Investigate mathematics and physics only for gate failures or suspicious defects, including affected consumers. Resolve all failures together; repeat check -> repair -> full battery until success. Immediately after the successful final battery, generate the concise report, then commit. Report/commit-dependent assertions belong inside those operations, with no post-commit content battery. Retain all mechanical conditions, liveness and repair mechanisms. Earlier Steps 1–5 retain their existing owner recertification protocol.

9. **Frontier scope.** Select item and page validators from the current run manifests. Never substitute whole-corpus checks or an empty passing selection. Load external suppliers as prerequisite context and retain relevant dependency-closure checks without requiring unrelated audits, formatting, or repairs. Respect frozen-frontier authority and guarded-recovery requirements in WORKFLOW.md; preserve history and refresh evidence for reopened work.

10. **Editing standard.** Communicate plainly and briefly. Replace stale code, prompts, and documentation cleanly. After major code changes, update relevant documentation and commit both. Keep proofs concise and sound, with essential hypotheses and caveats; add necessary intermediate lemmas. Follow SCHEMA.md formatting and run `node tools/proof-layout.mjs` on explicit changed item paths after the final edit or formatter.

11. **Context compaction.** Configure automatic compaction at 500,000 tokens for all agents through Codex settings and explicit worker arguments. Existing sessions need restart or resume to adopt changed settings. Before handoff or compaction, record the objective, verified state, blockers, and next action; recheck against disk on return. Do not store credentials or transcripts.

12. **Run-specific external-source boundary.** For `inverse-problems-knot-theory-all-109-2026-10-19`, the owner explicitly authorized precise external citations for remaining established dependencies across Step 1, after first applying this boundary to flyping/TOP. This overrides the local-proof-only rule in SCHEMA.md for this run only. Preserve resolved local proofs and dependencies. Clearly label every imported result as an external input; give its exact source, locator, hypotheses and actual reading limits. Do not describe it as a locally reconstructed proof, invent source coverage, or relabel an open problem as established. Ordinary source references and explicit Facts record these imports; no fabricated review, readiness or gate coverage is permitted. All ordinary workflow gates remain required. The required pause after `1-scaffold` gates and before Step 2 was satisfied. The owner subsequently authorized Step 2; its gates passed and the required pause before Step 3 was satisfied. The owner then authorized Step 3, continuous supervision, and committing and pushing completed work to `main` while the build continues. Retain native owner holds and all ordinary gates; checkpoints do not assert build completion or change draft publication status. The dated scope record is `research/inverse-problems-knot-theory-all-109-2026-10-19-supervision-20261020/external-source-closeout-direction.json`.

13. **Bounded reviewed-local provenance exception.** In the run named in rule 12,
root authorized preserving exactly the eight independently accepted local
lemmas listed in SCHEMA.md under truthful `ai-generated` statement/proof tags.
Only the exact `reviewed-local-lemma` role and root-owned
`local_proof_acceptance` mapping, actual independent-review file, and matching
accepted body SHA256 permit their lemma/dependency use. Root owns those item
metadata and digest bindings. Preserve actual source references, including
Hamilton/Brown adaptations in the composite boundary-link lemma; the exception
does not relabel its complete Statement as a published theorem. This exception
creates no external attribution,
mathematical acceptance, audit coverage, or waiver for other items; ordinary
workflow gates and frozen review histories remain required.
# Physics workspace agent instructions

Read ../CLAUDE.md fully for the general integrity and supervision rules. Within
this workspace, use SCHEMA.md and WORKFLOW.md; tools/physics-autopilot owns all
transitions. Root mathematics tools and briefs are unchanged and are not physics
authoring instructions. Use this workspace as cwd and keep all authored content,
plans, tasks, evidence, and state here.

Never edit outside this workspace or change imported mathematical snapshots.
Read research/math-imports.json to identify imports. Mathematical prerequisites may use published/unpublished items or planned results
in root mathematics prose scaffolds, with exact statements, hypotheses, proof
status and consumer mappings. Develop remaining missing prerequisites locally
with domain: mathematics and purely mathematical dependencies. Source permission
does not certify a plan as proved or authorize original mathematical edits.
Never repair a published original mathematical supplier from a physics run.

Apply the class-specific requirements in SCHEMA.md at every authoring, reader,
refuter, adjudication, judgment, and repair stage. Thought experiments have exactly
the same proof obligations as physics theorems. Postulates and experiments need
source and scope review, not invented deductive proofs. Never invent measurements,
full-text retrieval, audits, or certainty. Carry empirical uncertainties and
physical applicability conditions into every consuming physical conclusion.

A physical item's declared scope and empirical qualifications form part of its
public claim. Postulate formulations and experiment setup, procedure, observations,
uncertainty, and interpretation are also public interfaces. Changes to them require
direct and indirect consumer review under the general statement-change rule. Verification stamps,
citations, and proof-only changes alone do not propagate. Mathematical items retain
the original Statement/Definition interface rule.

Before authoring, judging, or adjudicating statistical physical claims, read the
statistical-evidence and double-slit section of ../PHYSICS-CONTENT-MODEL.md.
Experiments support or constrain specified models; finite data do not prove a
framework. Review confidence is not certainty about nature.

## Required mathematical rigor and explicit definitions

This rule applies to all physics frameworks, not only general relativity.
Be as mathematically rigorous and explicit as possible. Read “Mathematical rigor
and explicit definitions” in ../PHYSICS-CONTENT-MODEL.md. Provide explicit
mathematical definitions before using technical physical terms, with the objects,
spaces, maps, regularity, hypotheses, and conventions specified. Define locally
or use an exact earlier supplier and make its relevant definition explicit; do
not substitute terminology or a textbook citation for a definition. Reuse
established definitions without unnecessary repetition.

In particular, define reference frames and worldlines using the differential
geometry appropriate to the framework: specify spacetime structure, curve domain,
regularity, parameter and causal conditions where applicable, and whether a frame
means an adapted chart, observer congruence, or basis/tetrad field. Do not conflate
these notions or import relativistic assumptions into a nonrelativistic model.
Scaffolders and authors must supply missing mathematics locally or flag the exact
open prerequisite. Reviewers, judges, and adjudicators must check these definitions
and reject concealed prerequisite gaps or deductions resting on undefined terms.
Preserve the distinction between mathematical precision and empirical certainty.

## Current six-step numbering

For outside Codex repair agents in this physics workflow, use medium effort for Steps 1–3 and 6 and high effort for Steps 4–5; this supersedes the older numerical mapping in ../CLAUDE.md for physics only. Engine-dispatched Step-2 scope reviewers and repairers use the owner-selected GPT-6 Luna max profile and follow their own briefs. Step 1 performs initial authoring, Step 2 reviews scope/prerequisites, Step 3 is independent review, Step 4 is batch review/judging, Step 5 adjudicates/repairs, and Step 6 closes out.
# Physics library workspace

This workspace separates physics content from the existing mathematical library.
Planned subjects include Non-relativistic Classical Mechanics, Classical Electromagnetism, Non-relativistic Quantum
Mechanics, Special Relativity, General Relativity, and Thermodynamics.
Expanded research adds Fluid Dynamics, Relativistic Particle Mechanics,
Einstein–Maxwell Models, Quantum Field Theory including QED, Classical Statistical
Mechanics, Quantum Statistical Mechanics, and Thermodynamics and General
Relativity. The independent engine is
`../tools/physics-autopilot/`; its private helpers are `../tools/physics-support/`.
The tools symlink makes those commands available from this working directory.

Start with [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md), [SCHEMA.md](SCHEMA.md), and
[WORKFLOW.md](WORKFLOW.md). The agreed model and relation table are in
[../PHYSICS-CONTENT-MODEL.md](../PHYSICS-CONTENT-MODEL.md).

- items/: locally authored items and pinned mathematical supplier snapshots.
- library/: local A/B pages and imported canonical mathematics placements.
- briefs/: independent physics authoring, review, judging, and repair instructions.
- research/: physics plans, contracts, evidence, and import hashes.
- .physics-autopilot/: independent ignored run state.

Imported mathematical content retains its original IDs and bytes. It is runtime
supplier data, not independently authored physics content, and is excluded from
Git by the import-generated ignore block. Never edit imports. New mathematical
prerequisites may be developed locally with exclusively mathematical dependencies.
Physical items cannot enter mathematical pages or mathematical justification.

The workflow supports postulates, physics theorems, thought experiments, and
reported experiments. Physics theorems and thought experiments have identical
proof obligations; postulates and experiments require source and scope review.
Website integration, publication, and the first production content run remain
separate steps. See research/workflow-implementation-validation.json for checks
and inherited test-suite limitations.

Source research and proposed prose designs for the planned subjects are recorded in
[research/first-principles-2026-10-03/](research/first-principles-2026-10-03/README.md).
They record completed source reading, exact mathematical supplier mappings, local
arguments, and open prerequisites; they are not production readiness receipts.

The seven new five-agent teams and their scaffolds are indexed in
[research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/](research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/README.md).

Their future A/B pages and the first-principles frameworks have now been spliced
into [research/plan-spec.json](research/plan-spec.json), with complete source
links in the 13 `research/plan-*-track.md` design indexes. The
[splice record](research/prose-scaffold-splice.json) preserves source hashes,
proposed supplier homes and the geometry page split needed for acyclic ordering.
Item inventories are populated during the ordinary physics build stages.

The draft [Advanced QFT and supersymmetry scaffold](research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/README.md)
develops Non-Abelian gauge theory, effective field theory, supersymmetry,
localization, BPS states, generalized symmetries, defects and anomalies. Its
[discovery track](research/plan-advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry-track.md) and
separate plan fragment record proposed interfaces, exact supplier reuse and
remaining proof/source obligations; they have not been spliced into the active
canonical build plan or accepted as production content.

The canonical proof-item kind is `physics-theorem`, with the existing `pthm-`
prefix. `physical-theorem` is retired and rejected by current validators.
The website separates the Math Library and Physics Library; research scaffolds
do not become published physics pages merely by appearing in its subject index.
The Mathematical Universe excludes physics classes/domains and their mathematical
consumer chains at its loading boundary. The mathematics workflow remains separate.

The current six-step workflow and rebuild checklist are in [WORKFLOW.md](WORKFLOW.md) and [research/workflow-rebuild-plan.md](research/workflow-rebuild-plan.md).

Algorithms (`alg-`) are definition-like procedures; see [SCHEMA.md](SCHEMA.md#algorithms).

The [2026-10-11 published-only scaffold audits](research/scaffold-audit-2026-10-11/README.md) update classical mechanics, quantum mechanics, general relativity and thermodynamics. Their external mathematics uses published canonical suppliers; draft EM/SR physics is permitted only at its actual inspected scope. The later [owner-directed GR repair](research/gr-repair-2026-10-11/report.md) closes the three GR proof holds with local arguments for smooth splitting, generic dust evolution and MGHD gluing; source and production-acceptance limits remain recorded. These are research dispositions, not production readiness.

