+[A1] The bulk/boundary line-duality and its compatibility with bordism gluing are part of the adopted relative-theory data; this definition alone does not construct those data for any proposed model.
+
+[L1] Freed, Lecture 11, §§11.1–11.2, PDF pp. 154–163, gives the Pfaffian-line and invertible/relative-theory interface; the later thesis discussion in §11.4 is not used as a proved theorem.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 By [A1], $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{bulk}}(M,b|_M)$ is the dual of $\mathcal L_\partial(M,b|_M)$. Evaluation of a one-dimensional vector space against its dual is a well-defined nondegenerate pairing to $\mathbb C$, so the tensor of the two boundary values gives the stated scalar. [given, A1, algebra]
+
+2.1 Parallel transport on a dual line has the inverse scalar to parallel transport on the original line. Therefore the top transport in the displayed diagram preserves evaluation exactly when the product of the two holonomies is $1$, which is the stated cancellation condition. This is a definition of the scoped inflow interface, not an existence theorem for a bulk. [given, F1, F2, A1, L1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
