def-aqft-global-anomaly-holonomy Definition
Fix an even integer $d\ge2$, a compact smooth oriented Riemannian $d$-manifold $M$ without boundary with a specified spin structure, a smooth principal right $G$-bundle $P\to M$ with connection $A$, and a unitary finite-dimensional complex representation $R:G\to U(V_R)$. Let $E_R=P\times_R V_R$ be the associated Hermitian bundle. Let $S^+$ and $S^-$ be the associated bundles of the two half-spin representations of $\operatorname{Spin}(d)$. The chiral Dirac operator is the first-order operator

$$D^+_{A,R}:H^1(M;S^+\otimes E_R)\longrightarrow L^2(M;S^-\otimes E_R),$$

where $H^1$ and $L^2$ are the first Sobolev and square-integrable completions for the fixed Riemannian metric. Assume the operator is Fredholm. Its inverse determinant line is

$$\mathcal L_A=\left(\det_{\mathbb C}\ker D^+_{A,R}\right)^*\otimes\det_{\mathbb C}\operatorname{coker}D^+_{A,R},$$

with $\det_{\mathbb C}W=\bigwedge^{\dim_{\mathbb C}W}_{\mathbb C}W$. For a smooth family of such Fredholm operators over a parameter manifold, use the Quillen metric and the Bismut–Freed unitary connection on this line bundle. For a real or pseudoreal fermionic quadratic form, the Pfaffian line replaces the determinant line and ca

def-aqft-anomaly-inflow-relative-theory Definition
Fix an integer $d\ge1$. Let $Y$ be a compact oriented smooth spin $(d+1)$-manifold with boundary $M=\partial Y$, using the outward-normal-first boundary orientation. A background $b$ consists of the metric, spin structure, and any specified principal-bundle connections and associated fields on $Y$; its restriction to $M$ is the boundary background. A $d$-dimensional boundary theory is **relative to an invertible bulk theory** when its partition value is a vector $Z_\partial(M,b|_M)$ in a specified one-dimensional Hermitian line $\mathcal L_\partial(M,b|_M)$, and the bulk assigns the dual line $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{bulk}}(M,b|_M)\cong\mathcal L_\partial(M,b|_M)^*$ together with a vector $Z_{\mathrm{bulk}}(Y,b)$ in that line. The canonical evaluation

$$\mathrm{ev}:\mathcal L_\partial(M,b|_M)\otimes\mathcal L_{\mathrm{bulk}}(M,b|_M)\longrightarrow\mathbb C$$

then defines the combined scalar amplitude. Invertibility means that the bulk assigns a one-dimensional line to each closed $d$-dimensional boundary background and an isomorphism to each bordism map; orientation reversal replaces the assigned line by its dual. For a loop $\gamma$ of compatible background fields and endpoint gauge

pthm-aqft-global-anomaly-test Statement
Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Let $M=S^4$ with its boundary orientation and standard spin structure, and let $E\to M$ be a smooth rank-two Hermitian bundle with structure group $SU(2)$ and smooth unitary connection $A$. Treat $SU(2)$ as a nondynamical background global symmetry and consider one massless left-handed Weyl fermion in the fundamental representation. Use the inverse determinant/Pfaffian line and the index-density normalization $I_6=[\widehat A(TM)\operatorname{ch}(E)]_6$ for its universal local anomaly polynomial, with $F_A$ anti-Hermitian and $\operatorname{ch}(E)=\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf 2}\exp(-F_A/(2\pi i))$. Assume the selected background is in the index-one sector, meaning the coupled chiral Dirac operator $D_A^+$ is Fredholm and $\operatorname{ind}_{\mathbb C}D_A^+=1$. Let $z=-I\in SU(2)$ act on the fermion fibers, and form the corresponding order-two loop $\gamma_z$ of the background/gauge-field groupoid; its mapping torus is $Y_z=S^4\times S^1$ with the pullback connection and periodic fermions around $S^1$. For $N\ge1$ independent identical Weyl doublets, the local polynomial vanishes and the boundary holonomy on this loop is $(-1)^N$. Adopt, for this test,

ex-aqft-anomaly-inflow-example Example
Take the index-one Euclidean $S^4$ background, one massless left-handed Weyl doublet of a nondynamical $SU(2)$ global symmetry, and the order-two central transformation $z=-I$ specified in the global-anomaly test. Assume the source-supported chiral index is $1$ and pair the boundary determinant/Pfaffian line with the five-dimensional bulk whose test-loop phase is $(-1)^{\operatorname{ind}_2D_5}$. The boundary line has character $\chi(1)=1$, $\chi(z)=-1$; the mapping-torus mod-two index is $1$, so the bulk character is $\chi^{-1}=\chi$ and the combined character is trivial. The local index-density anomaly polynomial is zero. For the finite normalization check take a single-vertex complex $K$ and the ordinary field groupoid $\mathcal F(K)=BC_2$, with one object and automorphism group $C_2$. The combined unit weight is arrow-invariant and its normalized groupoid sum is $1/2$. Without the bulk, the odd boundary character is not a scalar invariant weight and no such finite gauging sum is defined. For two identical copies the boundary character itself is trivial and the same normalized groupoid sum applies. This example tests only the stated loop and does not establish that even copies h

def-qftx-finite-groupoid-integration Definition
A **finite groupoid** is a small category in which every morphism is invertible, with finitely many isomorphism classes and a finite automorphism group at every object. Write $\pi_0(\mathcal C)$ for its set of isomorphism classes. Its **groupoid cardinality** is

$$|\mathcal C|:=\sum_{[c]\in\pi_0(\mathcal C)}\frac{1}{|\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathcal C}(c)|}.$$

For an isomorphism-invariant function $f:\pi_0(\mathcal C)\to\mathbb C$, define

$$\int_{c\in\mathcal C} f(c):=\sum_{[c]\in\pi_0(\mathcal C)}\frac{f(c)}{|\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathcal C}(c)|}.$$

For finite groupoids $\mathcal C,\mathcal D$, a functor $p:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D$, and an object $d$ of $\mathcal D$, its **homotopy fiber** $\mathcal C_d$ has objects $(c,\alpha)$ with $\alpha:p(c)\overset{\cong}{\longrightarrow}d$ and morphisms $u:(c,\alpha)\to(c',\alpha')$ satisfying $\alpha'\circ p(u)=\alpha$. Define pullback and pushforward of invariant functions by

$$(p^*g)([c])=g([p(c)]),\qquad (p_!f)([d])=\int_{(c,\alpha)\in\mathcal C_d} f([c]).$$

A span $\mathcal X\xleftarrow{a}\mathcal E\xrightarrow{b}\mathcal Y$ of finite groupoids therefore induces the linear map $b_!a^*$ on invariant functions.



