on large-mass Pauli–Villars subtraction scheme and the consistent effective-action convention fixed in the model data.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Adel Bilal, Lectures on Anomalies (full lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0802.0634","locator":"§§5.2–5.2.4, 7.1–7.2, 9.4, and 10.4, printed pp. 31–39, 45–50, 75–77, and 87–88: the Pauli–Villars-regulated chiral triangle, representation trace, local descent, and exact anomaly-polynomial convention."},{"title":"Enrique Alvarez, Gauge Anomalies","url":"https://members.ift.uam-csic.es/ealvarez/anomalies.ea.pdf","locator":"§§2.1–2.4, printed pp. 25–38: independent descent, representation additivity/conjugation, and consistent-counterterm convention."}]},"library":"physics","proof_strategy":"direct","physical_scope":"A conditional one-loop result for infinitesimal background transformations of the stated flat-space SU(3)×U(1) global symmetry in one common large-mass Pauli–Villars subtraction convention. It proves only vanishing of the perturbative local class for the conjugate pair; it does not test global anomalies, establish a dynamical gauge theory, or construct a continuum QFT."}]},{"order":997,"id":"qftx-thooft-anomalies-examples","kind":"B","category":"advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry","library":"physics","title":"Perturbative ’t Hooft Anomalies and Counterterms — Examples","companion":"qftx-thooft-anomalies","requires":["qftx-thooft-anomalies"],"items":[{"id":"ex-aqft-single-chiral-fundamental-polynomial","kind":"example","domain":"physics","title":"A single chiral fundamental has a nonzero local polynomial","reference":"items/ex-aqft-single-chiral-fundamental-polynomial.md","design_state":"Step 1 authored; current interface, source evidence, and readiness are recorded in batch-41 notes.","dependency_level":2,"deps":["def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data","def-aqft-cubic-anomaly-polynomial"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data":"formulation-prerequisite","def-aqft-cubic-anomaly-polynomial":"mathematical-premise"},"statement":"Use the same flat-space background convention but take the spectrum to contain only one positive-chirality species $E=(\\mathbf3,+1)$. Its polynomial is","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Adel Bilal, Lectures on Anomalies (full lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0802.0634","locator":"§§7.1–7.2, 9.4, and 10.4, printed pp. 45–50, 75–77, and 87–88: positive-chirality trace coefficient and descent polynomial."},{"title":"Enrique Alvarez, Gauge Anomalies","url":"https://members.ift.uam-csic.es/ealvarez/anomalies.ea.pdf","locator":"§§2.2–2.3, printed pp. 30–36: the cubic representation index and chiral spectrum anomaly polynomial."}]},"library":"physics","proof_strategy":"direct","physical_scope":"A worked check of the formal one-loop polynomial for one positive-chirality (3,+1) species; it does not assert that its local cohomology class is nontrivial, that all counterterms fail, or that any global anomaly is present."},{"id":"ex-aqft-mixed-background-counterterm-shift","kind":"example","domain":"physics","title":"A mixed-background counterterm shifts the local representative","reference":"items/ex-aqft-mixed-background-counterterm-shift.md","design_state":"Step 1 authored; current interface, source evidence, and readiness are recorded in batch-41 notes.","dependency_level":2,"deps":["def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data","def-aqft-thooft-anomaly","lem-aqft-local-counterterm-shift-is-relative-coboundary"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data":"formulation-prerequisite","def-aqft-thooft-anomaly":"formulation-prerequisite","lem-aqft-local-counterterm-shift-is-relative-coboundary":"mathematical-premise"},"statement":"Let $A$ be a smooth compactly supported anti-Hermitian $\\mathfrak{su}(3)$ connection one-form on trivial $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, with curvature $F_A=dA+A\\wedge A$, and let $B$ be a smooth real $U(1)_V$ background one-form with compactly supported gauge parameter $\\alpha$. In natural units, $[A]=[B]=\\text{mass}$ and $[F_A]=\\text{mass}^2$. Define the local three-form","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Adel Bilal, Lectures on Anomalies (full lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0802.0634","locator":"§§8.3 and 9.2–9.4, printed pp. 63–77: local Chern–Simons descent and the anomaly/counterterm ambiguity."},{"title":"Enrique Alvarez, Gauge Anomalies","url":"https://members.ift.uam-csic.es/ealvarez/anomalies.ea.pdf","locator":"§§2.1–2.4, printed pp. 25–38: Chern–Simons forms, local counterterm shifts, and the distinction between consistent and covariant anomalies."}]},"library":"physics","proof_strategy":"direct","physical_scope":"A local counterterm calculation on trivial flat-space SU(3) and U(1) backgrounds with compact support. It records one infinitesimal U(1) variation and does not claim that the full combined anomaly class vanishes or that a global counterterm exists on nontrivial bundles."},{"id":"ex-aqft-vectorlike-conjugate-chiral-pair","kind":"example","domain":"physics","title":"A conjugate SU(3)×U(1) chiral pair with zero local polynomial","reference":"items/ex-aqft-vectorlike-conjugate-chiral-pair.md","design_state":"Step 1 authored; current interface, source evidence, and readiness are recorded in batch-41 notes.","dependency_level":3,"deps":["def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data","def-aqft-cubic-anomaly-polynomial","lem-aqft-dual-cubic-anomaly-polynomial-cancellation","pthm-aqft-selected-chiral-spectrum-anomaly-cancellation"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data":"formulation-prerequisite","def-aqft-cubic-anomaly-polynomial":"mathematical-premise","lem-aqft-dual-cubic-anomaly-polynomial-cancellation":"mathematical-premise","pthm-aqft-selected-chiral-spectrum-anomaly-cancellation":"physical-result"},"statement":"Use the flat four-dimensional background data with global group $SU(3)\\times U(1)_V$ and the common consistent regulator fixed above. Take the finite spectrum to consist of positive-chirality Weyl species $E=(\\mathbf3,+1)$ and $E^*=(\\bar{\\mathbf3},-1)$. On a formal six-dimensional extension, write the anti-Hermitian $SU(3)$ curvature as $F_A$ and the $U(1)_V$ curvature as $f=dB$. The defining-representation curvature is $F_E=F_A+i f I_3$, with $\\operatorname{tr}F_A=0$, so","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Adel Bilal, Lectures on Anomalies (full lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0802.0634","locator":"§§7.1–7.2, 9.4, and 10.4, printed pp. 45–50, 75–77, and 87–88: additivity, conjugate-representation traces, and the local descent normalization."},{"title":"Enrique Alvarez, Gauge Anomalies","url":"https://members.ift.uam-csic.es/ealvarez/anomalies.ea.pdf","locator":"§§2.2–2.3, printed pp. 30–36: linear descent and representation-index cancellation."}]},"library":"physics","proof_strategy":"direct","physical_scope":"A worked algebraic check of the flat-space one-loop background model with positive-chirality species (3,+1) and (bar 3,-1); no global-anomaly, dynamical-gauge, empirical, or continuum claim is made."}]}]

