+Pair: `qftx-global-anomalies-inflow` / `qftx-global-anomalies-inflow-examples`
+
+## Plan and dispatch evidence
+
+The plan-spec row at `research/plan-spec.json` and the complete design section at `research/plan-advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry-track.md` agree on the A/B IDs, title, order 998, category/library, and the two page prerequisites `qftx-thooft-anomalies` and `qftx-finite-symmetry-gauging`; no plan/design conflict was found. The planned item IDs are the two definitions, one global-anomaly physics theorem, and one B-page example. The current dependency-level record gives batch 42 level 7 with batch 14 and batch 41 as suppliers. These are authoring priorities, not a wait barrier.
+
+The current batch-41 manifest has the `qftx-thooft-anomalies` A/B rows but both item arrays are empty. `def-aqft-thooft-anomaly` has no current item carrier, and `loadStep1Record`/`step1Decision` report its readiness record missing. Its exact planned interface is a background-gauge variation modulo specified local counterterms, separating dynamical-gauge inconsistency from explicit symmetry breaking. The consumer use is conditional on that interface; no upstream carrier or proof is asserted.
+
+The current batch-14 A page `qftx-finite-symmetry-gauging` contains `def-aqft-background-gauging-data`, `thm-aqft-finite-group-gauging`, and `thm-aqft-finite-two-group-gauging-gluing`. The current readiness records for its ordinary groupoid Definition and theorem are owner-recorded `ready` and their hashes are current. I read their exact statements and proofs, including the finite-groupoid automorphism weights, the invariant-weight hypothesis, and the finite homotopy-pullback gluing assumptions. The ordinary finite-groupoid model does not establish a continuum anomaly or QFT. The two-group branch is not used by this pair. The finite `BC2` normalization uses the narrower published local item `def-qftx-finite-groupoid-integration`, whose complete Definition and proof were also inspected; the required page edge to batch 14 remains a scoped page prerequisite.
+
+## Completed item: `def-aqft-global-anomaly-holonomy`
+
+**Claim and conventions.** Defines the inverse determinant-line holonomy for a smooth Fredholm family of chiral Dirac operators on compact closed even-dimensional Riemannian spin manifolds, with a fixed unitary representation, Quillen metric, Bismut–Freed connection, endpoint gauge identification, and fixed regularization/counterterm convention. The phase is in $U(1)$. Its planned direct supplier is `def-aqft-thooft-anomaly`, currently absent as above, so this item remains conditional.
+
+**Dependencies.** `def-aqft-thooft-anomaly` (formulation prerequisite; planned supplier, missing carrier). Planned `dependency_level: 1` because the exact planned upstream definition has no in-run suppliers.
+
+**Sources read.** Dai–Freed, *Eta-Invariants and Determinant Lines*, §§1–2 and §5, PDF pp. 1–3, 8–15, 31–35, especially the inverse determinant line and the proof identifying adiabatic eta parallel transport with the Bismut–Freed connection: <https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9405012>. Freed, *Lectures on Field Theory and Topology*, Lecture 11 §§11.1–11.2, PDF pp. 154–163, for Pfaffian/determinant lines and anomaly-line context: <https://abel.math.harvard.edu/~dafr/CBMS133.pdf>. The lecture notes explicitly distinguish their rigorous sections from the thesis discussion in §11.4; no claim from §11.4 is used as a proved theorem. Both full-body sources are now stamped in the batch source-coverage record.
+
+**Proof status and next action.** The local proof checks finite-dimensional determinant-line fibers and that unitary parallel transport plus the endpoint gauge arrow gives a frame-independent unit phase. Next: author `def-aqft-anomaly-inflow-relative-theory`, then the conditional SU(2) phase/inflow theorem and worked B example; record exact supplier excerpts, source retrieval stamps, proof contracts, and readiness dispositions after all four carriers are present.
+
+## Completed item: `def-aqft-anomaly-inflow-relative-theory`
+
+**Claim and conventions.** Defines a spin boundary/bulk pair using the outward-normal-first boundary orientation. The bulk boundary line is the dual of the boundary anomaly line, and cancellation on a specified background loop is the equation `Hol_boundary * Hol_bulk = 1`. A `tikzcd` square illustrates the evaluation/parallel-transport condition; the proof states the line-duality argument without relying on the diagram. No bulk existence is asserted.
+
+**Dependencies.** `def-aqft-global-anomaly-holonomy` (same-batch definition, level 1) and `def-aqft-thooft-anomaly` (planned batch-41 formulation prerequisite, exact item currently absent). Its planned level is 2. The missing batch-41 definition remains an explicit unresolved input.
+
+**Sources read.** Freed, *Lectures on Field Theory and Topology*, Lecture 11 §§11.1–11.2, PDF pp. 154–163, for Pfaffian lines and the relative/invertible-theory interface; Dai–Freed, §5, PDF pp. 31–35, for determinant-line parallel transport and holonomy. §11.4 of Freed is identified as a thesis section and is not used as a proved result. Full-body source-fetch stamps are recorded for both documents.
+
